   ACTION
      [7s] From seconds 7–9, water enters red-kidney-bean from the surrounding wet perlite. Increase the seed body's dimensions gradually by approximately twenty percent. Keep the swollen coat visibly present and intact throughout this interval; reduce the surrounding water film slightly without introducing droplets, a stream or a pouring source.
      [9s] From seconds 9–11, a small opening forms beside the hilum and the radicle emerges through it, extending downward to z=-0.010. Retain the rest of the swollen seed coat. No leafy shoot is present during this interval.
      [11s] From seconds 11–13, lengthen the main root downward to z=-0.035 while the hypocotyl extends upward and lifts the seed body. The cotyledons remain enclosed within the split coat until the end of this interval.
      [13s] From seconds 13–15, extend the main root to z=-0.045 and grow two short lateral roots on each side. Expose the paired cotyledons at z=0.025 while retaining split coat around their bases. Extend the shoot upward to z=0.045 and let two tightly folded unifoliate leaves peek out above the cotyledons. All roots, stem tissue, cotyledons and leaves remain connected parts of red-kidney-bean.
   NARRATION
      [7s] "A seed takes in water. Roots grow downward, and a shoot reaches up toward the light."
